asily go on a  couple of years waxing and waning and  dr. Peter  has is a vaccine scientist at Baylor  College of Medicine in Houston he  believes governments need a strategy for  how daily life will operate for the next  two years yes two years that's the  length of time it could take to develop  tests and distribute a vaccine once the  surge in cases is over do you see us  potentially getting to a place where  restrictions and social distancing are  loosened and then tightened up as  localized outbreaks pop up yeah that's  that's a possible scenario but how do  you operationalize that and and and how  do you inform companies or universities  when to stay open when to close and how  do you get people to feel comfortable  doing that who might be terrified about  resuming normal daily activity so a lot  of complexities without a lot of  precedent the complexity is not lost on  public officials anywhere they've  struggled to explain what a way out  might look like if back to normal means  acting like there never was a  coronavirus problem I don't think that's  gonna happen until we do have a  situation where you can completely  protect the population I think even  after we're through this first wave we  will need to remain vigilant and we will  need to bring in different measures  normality as it was before will not come  back full-on until we get a vaccine for  this  in South Korea they've learned that  social distancing is only half the  battle the government implemented an  aggressive system of testing contact  tracing public alerts and quarantine to  keep case numbers and fatalities  relatively low even then life is nowhere  close to returning to what it once was  the virus keeps popping up other  countries have begun to relax  restrictions only to experience a second  wave of infection Canada the US and  Europe are still at step 1 trying to  contain the outbreak and public health  officials say they're nowhere close to  being ready to think about anything else  how are we going to get back to normal  without higher levels of testing like if  people are walking around with this  virus that they don't even know it right  now our focus is to tackle this actual  outbreak at some point in the near  future we'll be looking at how do we  apply serologic testing for example to  look at the level of immunity in the  population even though everything shut  down all at once it may come back slowly  in waves once the peak of cases is over  perhaps restaurants and bars reopen with  fewer seats inside schools could come  next big concerts and sporting events  are likely a long way off dr. ali mock  dodd worked to develop the pandemic  models used by the white house so until  we have a vaccine our definition of  normal should be totally different what  normal is so I don't expect all of us to  be back to normal as 100% immediately  but in a staged approach to make sure  the virus is not circulating and we  don't allow this virus to have a second  wave the risk is in coming back too fast  there's little room for trial and error  when human lives are on the line dr.  Lena when is the former public health  commissioner for the city of Baltimore I  don't think that you can lift it over  all all together and say let's go back  to exactly what our Priya komen 19 days  were like that's not going to work  because we know what the consequence is  going to be we're going to have a huge  number of deaths and koba 19 will spiral  out of control  almost every expert agrees a vaccine is  the fastest safest way to get our world  back even if it means a year or more of  waiting until that happens we're stuck  in a cat and mouse game with something  that can't be seen or detected until  it's too late it may be the most painful  weight of our lives so we're in this for  a while we are and it is a new normal  that we all have to get used to  recognizing how difficult this is and  how many sacrifices are going to be  borne by everyone but if we stop down  th